WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:06.160
Daenerys Targaryen, played by Emilia Clarke,&nbsp;
quickly became one of the most popular characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:10.880
in "Game of Thrones." Audiences hopped on&nbsp;
board her hell-bent mission to bring her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:15.840
rage to Westeros and oust the LanniSsters&nbsp;
from the seat of power, the Iron Throne,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:20.240
which Daenerys believes is her birthright.&nbsp;
The show spends plenty of time developing her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:24.937
character in a way that made audiences feel&nbsp;
incredibly sympathetic towards her cause.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.937 --> 00:00:28.172
She’s undoubtedly one of the&nbsp;
show's most beloved heroes.

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:30.148
“I need to be more than that.”

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:35.760
Once she fully came into her own, commanding&nbsp;
armies and fighting for justice, Daenerys was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:40.160
indisputably, a brilliant and compelling&nbsp;
character. Not to mention the addition of her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:45.920
dragons, which only made fans invest in her quest&nbsp;
even further. While it was obvious what producers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:51.040
D.B. Weiss and David Benioff were trying to&nbsp;
say about the allure that true power holds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:57.280
and how it easily corrupts people, one particular&nbsp;
scene took things too far. This is, of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:01.600
when Daenerys burned King's Landing.&nbsp;
It’s a moment that, unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:06.080
turned fans against Daenerys in the&nbsp;
final season of "Game of Thrones."

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:10.880
Okay, sure - Daenerys burned people alive&nbsp;
using her dragons a number of times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:15.760
but it's the intention behind the destruction&nbsp;
of King's Landing that just took things too far.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:19.280
Now, we can admit that Daenerys&nbsp;
was in a bad place. Jon Snow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:23.143
played by Kit Harrington, rejected her&nbsp;
because he discovers they're related.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:23.929
Yikes. 

00:01:24.071 --> 00:01:28.320
She loses both her closest confidante&nbsp;
Missandei, played by Nathalie Emmanuel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:33.600
and one of her two remaining dragons to Cersei's&nbsp;
forces. Daenerys has nothing left to lose,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:39.749
which leads to the complete destruction of King's&nbsp;
Landing in Season 8, Episode 5, "The Bells."

00:01:39.749 --> 00:01:44.240
It's completely Daenerys' choice to&nbsp;
willingly burn probably thousands of innocent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:49.120
people - including innocents, women, and children&nbsp;
- alive in the streets. The civilians' collective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:54.480
terror is truly felt throughout the episode, with&nbsp;
a war happening on the ground and a fire-breathing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:59.520
dragon hovering in the sky. At this point,&nbsp;
Daenerys is no longer a liberator. It's a complete&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:05.854
departure from and betrayal of the woman who was&nbsp;
so dedicated to freeing slaves for the past 7&nbsp;seasons. &nbsp;

00:02:05.854 --> 00:02:11.143
When faced with King's Landing, Daenerys&nbsp;
kills thousands of people simply because she can.

00:02:11.143 --> 00:02:16.320
As the battle rages, Jon is horrified by her&nbsp;
actions when he sees the burnt bodies of men,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:21.280
women, and children litter the ground. It's&nbsp;
only further hammered home that Dany is truly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:25.840
off the deep end when she announces in the&nbsp;
Season Finale, "The Iron Throne," that she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:31.360
was planning to extend her wrath across the rest&nbsp;
of the world. Jon ultimately faces an impossible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:37.280
choice and kills his queen, aunt, and lover&nbsp;
right in front of the throne she wants so badly.

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:40.720
There's no question that Daenerys,&nbsp;
a hot-blooded Targaryen with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:45.200
family history of mental instability and&nbsp;
massive dragons willing to do her bidding,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:49.360
made some troubling decisions throughout&nbsp;
"Thrones." Still, viewers could always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:55.360
be comforted that she was on the right side of&nbsp;
justice. That is, until the show's final season.

00:02:55.360 --> 00:03:00.160
The creative minds behind "Game of Thrones"&nbsp;
maintain that the Daenerys twist was planned all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:04.880
along, and it makes sense with her character.&nbsp;
However, the Daenerys that audiences fell for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:10.960
would never have killed innocents. Though Clarke&nbsp;
initially defended the finale back in May of 2019,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:15.040
saying Daenerys' destruction was a&nbsp;
logical route for the character to take,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:20.960
she has since reversed course. During a&nbsp;
June 2021 chat with MTV News correspondent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:24.240
Josh Horowitz on his podcast, Clarke said,

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:27.257
"I get why people were pissed. I totally get it.”

00:03:27.257 --> 00:03:32.480
Though Clarke said she did have some, quote,&nbsp;
"autonomy" over Daenerys' character arc, when all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:38.412
was said and done, she just had to play what was&nbsp;
given to her as truthfully as possible. She added,

00:03:38.412 --> 00:03:42.800
"The editor decides what it looks like, and&nbsp;
the writer decides what I'm saying. So you just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:48.400
gotta go in and try and give it as much truth and&nbsp;
honesty [...] and yourself as you possibly can."

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:53.680
